        "why": "The order of the organs IS the answer: food passes crop, then gizzard, then midgut, then hindgut, and a drawing with the gizzard before the crop is wrong even when every name is spelled right.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Putting the gizzard before the crop. The crop comes first and stores the food; the gizzard behind it grinds it.",
          "Drawing the hepatic caecae at the end of the midgut. They arise at its front end, where it joins the gizzard.",
          "Leaving out the Malpighian tubules at the midgut-hindgut junction."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Draw one long tube top to bottom first, then hang the side pieces on it: glands by the crop, caecae at the midgut front, tubules at the midgut-hindgut junction.",
        "margin_note": "Label at least the crop, gizzard, hepatic caecae, mesenteron, Malpighian tubules, ileum, colon and rectum — the labels carry the two marks."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_foregut",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Foregut (stomodaeum)",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Region 1",
        "lines": [
          "Foregut (stomodaeum): pharynx, oesophagus,",
          "crop and gizzard.",
          "The pharynx is a short tube. It leads into a",
          "narrow tubular oesophagus.",
          "The oesophagus opens into a sac-like crop,",
          "which stores the food.",
          "Behind the crop lies a thick-walled muscular",
          "gizzard. Its chitinous lining bears six teeth",
          "that grind the food, so it works as a mill",
          "and a sieve.",
          "A funnel-shaped stomodeal valve projects into",
          "the midgut and stops food moving back."
        ],
        "why": "The foregut is the storing and grinding part, so each organ is described by the job it does — sac for storage, muscular grinder with six teeth, and a one-way valve at the exit.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the crop grinds the food. The crop only stores it; the gizzard grinds it.",
          "Giving the wrong number of gizzard teeth. There are six chitinous teeth.",
          "Leaving out the stomodeal valve, which is a named part of this region."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Foregut = PECG: pharynx, oesophagus, crop, gizzard — store first, grind next.",
        "margin_note": "Four organs, then one line each for the crop, the gizzard's six teeth and the stomodeal valve. This is the biggest region: two marks."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_midgut",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Midgut (mesenteron)",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Region 2",
        "lines": [
          "Midgut (mesenteron): a short narrow tube.",
          "At its front end are 6 to 8 finger-like",
          "diverticula called hepatic caecae.",
          "The front part of the midgut secretes enzymes",
          "and the hind part absorbs the digested food.",
          "The food is wrapped in a porous peritrophic",
          "membrane. It protects the midgut wall from",
          "hard food particles."
        ],
        "why": "The midgut is where digestion and absorption actually happen, so the answer must state both jobs and name the two structures that serve them — the hepatic caecae and the peritrophic membrane.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Calling the hepatic caecae excretory. They are digestive: they secrete enzymes and absorb food.",
          "Writing that the whole midgut absorbs. The front part secretes; the hind part absorbs."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Mesenteron is where digestion finishes: the caecae add enzymes at the front, the wall absorbs at the back.",
        "margin_note": "Short tube, 6 to 8 hepatic caecae, secretory front and absorptive back, peritrophic membrane. Four facts, one mark."
      },
      {
        "id": "s5_hindgut",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Hindgut (proctodaeum)",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Region 3",
        "lines": [
          "Hindgut (proctodaeum): a long coiled tube.",
          "It has three parts — ileum, colon and rectum.",
          "A sphincter between midgut and hindgut stops",
          "food and uric acid from moving back.",
          "The ileum collects uric acid from the",
          "Malpighian tubules (about 100 to 150) at the",
          "midgut-hindgut junction.",
          "The rectum bears six rectal papillae that",
          "absorb water from the undigested food.",
          "The rectum opens out through the anus."
        ],
        "why": "The hindgut recovers water and passes out waste, so the ileum, colon and rectum are described by what each one collects or absorbs, ending at the anus where the canal ends.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the Malpighian tubules open into the colon. They open at the anterior end of the ileum.",
          "Forgetting the six rectal papillae, which are the water-absorbing folds of the rectum."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Hindgut = ICR: ileum takes the uric acid, colon carries, rectum takes back the water.",
        "margin_note": "Three parts in order, the sphincter, the Malpighian tubules and the rectal papillae. One mark."
      },
